c:["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-2","children":"Mixed hearing loss involves both conductive and sensorineural components and requires a combined treatment approach."}]
d:["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-2","children":"A full audiological evaluation — including Pure Tone Audiogram (PTA), Speech Audiogram, and Tympanometry — is performed at the clinic to classify the type and degree of hearing loss before any treatment is recommended."}]
e:["$","div",null,{"className":"border-b border-gray-200 pb-8","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-teal-700 mb-3","children":"H3: Tinnitus (Ringing in the Ears)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Tinnitus is the perception of sound — ringing, buzzing, hissing, or whooshing — when no external sound is present. It affects millions of people and can range from a mild nuisance to a severely disruptive condition that impairs concentration, sleep, and emotional wellbeing."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-2","children":"Tinnitus is a symptom, not a disease in itself. It can arise from noise-induced cochlear damage, middle ear pathology, Eustachian tube dysfunction, Menière's disease, cardiovascular conditions, or certain medications. Identifying the underlying cause is the first step — and Dr. kushwah's structured evaluation helps pinpoint it accurately, guiding targeted treatment rather than symptomatic suppression alone."}]]}]
f:["$","div",null,{"className":"border-b border-gray-200 pb-8","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-teal-700 mb-3","children":"H3: Otosclerosis and Conductive Hearing Loss"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Otosclerosis is a condition in which abnormal bone growth in the middle ear — particularly around the stapes (stirrup bone) — prevents the ossicles from vibrating freely. This results in progressive conductive hearing loss that is often bilateral and worsens over time."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-2","children":"Stapedotomy — a precise microsurgical procedure to bypass the fixed stapes and restore sound conduction — is performed by Dr. kushwah with excellent outcomes. Most patients experience significant hearing improvement within weeks of surgery."}]]}]
10:["$","div",null,{"className":"border-b border-gray-200 pb-8","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-teal-700 mb-3","children":"H3: Menière's Disease"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Menière's disease is a chronic condition of the inner ear characterised by episodes of severe vertigo lasting minutes to hours, fluctuating low-frequency hearing loss, tinnitus, and a sensation of fullness or pressure in the affected ear. It is believed to be caused by abnormal fluid pressure within the endolymphatic system of the inner ear."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-2","children":"Management includes dietary modifications (low-sodium diet), medications to control vertigo episodes, and in refractory cases, procedures such as intratympanic steroid or gentamicin injections, or endolymphatic sac decompression surgery."}]]}]
11:["$","div",null,{"className":"border-b border-gray-200 pb-8","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-teal-700 mb-3","children":"H3: Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss (SSNHL)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Sudden sensorineural hearing loss is a medical emergency — a rapid, unexplained loss of hearing, typically in one ear, that occurs within 72 hours. It requires urgent assessment and treatment, as delay significantly worsens prognosis."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-2","children":"If you or someone you know experiences sudden hearing loss, especially accompanied by tinnitus or dizziness, contact Dr. kushwah's clinic immediately. Early treatment with corticosteroids (oral or intratympanic) offers the best chance of recovery."}]]}]
12:["$","div",null,{"className":"border-b border-gray-200 pb-8","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-teal-700 mb-3","children":"H3: Swimmer's Ear (Otitis Externa)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Otitis externa is an infection or inflammation of the outer ear canal, commonly triggered by water exposure, skin conditions (eczema, psoriasis), or repeated trauma from cotton buds. Symptoms include ear pain, itching, discharge, and blocked hearing."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-2","children":"Management involves thorough ear cleaning (aural toilet), topical antibiotic or antifungal drops, and in severe cases, wick insertion to deliver medication deep into the canal."}]]}]
13:["$","div",null,{"className":"border-b border-gray-200 pb-8","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-teal-700 mb-3","children":"H3: Earwax Buildup (Cerumen Impaction)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"While earwax is a natural protective secretion, excessive accumulation can cause blocked hearing, earache, tinnitus, and dizziness. Dr. kushwah performs safe, professional microsuction or syringing to remove impacted wax — avoiding the risks of self-removal with cotton buds or ear drops alone."}]]}]
14:["$","div",null,{"className":"pb-8","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-teal-700 mb-3","children":"H3: Congenital Ear Conditions (Microtia, Atresia)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Some children are born with structural abnormalities of the outer or middle ear — including microtia (underdeveloped pinna) and aural atresia (absent or narrowed ear canal). These can cause significant conductive hearing loss from birth."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-2","children":"Early audiological assessment and — where appropriate — bone-anchored hearing devices (BAHA or Bonebridge) or surgical reconstruction are evaluated and planned as part of a child-centred, family-involved care pathway."}]]}]
16:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl shadow-sm","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-800","children":"How do I know if my child has a hearing problem?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 mt-2","children":"Signs in infants include not reacting to loud sounds, not turning toward voices, and not babbling by 6 months. In older children, watch for not responding when called, asking for things to be repeated, turning the TV up too loud, or falling behind in speech. Bring your child for a hearing evaluation without delay if you notice any of these signs."}]]}]
19:null
